TEXTS = {
    "lead_opening": "Welcome.\nNow let's build your organization's fundraising strategy.\nBefore we go ahead, there is something very important I want you to understand.\nTo raise money consistently and exponentially, you need to build your organization's fundraising system.\nBasically, a system that runs consistently to bring in money for your organization.\nAnd your fundraising system is made up of two parts.\nThe first is your fundraising strategy.\nYour fundraising strategy identifies the exact type of people, businesses and grantors meant to fund your mission, where to consistently find them, how to attract them and the step-by-step process to raise money from them.\nThen you identify the people, technology, materials, resources and content you need to execute that strategy.\nThe second part is the actual system that runs that strategy consistently.\nThat's where technology, your board, volunteers, staff or a fundraising team can actually help you execute.\nIn this game, we're starting with the foundation, your fundraising strategy.\nAnd we're going to build it around the fundraising goal you can see on your screen.\nBy the time you're done, you will understand what it takes to raise money and build your organization's fundraising system.\nAnd your ideas will become part of the strategy your organization can use to reach this fundraising goal.\nDon't worry about trying to give perfect answers.\nI'm going to walk you through it one step at a time.\nReady?\nLet's get started.",
    "board_opening": "Welcome.\nYou are about to help your organization build its fundraising strategy.\nAnd before we start, I want you to understand why your ideas matter.\nTo raise money consistently and exponentially, your organization needs a fundraising system.\nAnd the foundation of that system is the fundraising strategy.\nThat's where you identify the exact type of people, businesses and grantors meant to fund the mission, where to find them, how to attract them and the process to raise money from them.\nNow, one person can sit down and try to figure all this out alone.\nBut the strategy will only be as strong as that person's ideas and experience.\nThis is why we are getting everybody involved.\nThe more people who play this game, the more ideas your organization has on how to raise money and the more support it has when raising money and building the system that drives the strategy.\nWe believe people who plan together execute together.\nSo as you play, you're not simply answering questions.\nYou're learning how fundraising works, sharing your own wisdom and helping your organization build a strategy you can all understand and execute together.\nDon't worry about giving perfect answers.\nI'll walk you through it.\nLet's start.",
    "a1_intro": "Let's start with the most important question.\nWho should actually fund your mission?\nThis is important because not everybody is meant to fund your organization.\nOne of the mistakes organizations make is simply saying, we need donors, we need businesses or we need foundations.\nThat's too broad.\nThere are specific people who have a strong reason to care about the problem you solve.\nThere are specific businesses that have a reason to care about the people you serve, the community you work in or the outcome you're trying to create.\nAnd there are grantors whose funding priorities already align with your mission.\nSo your job is to find your people.\nBefore I guide you deeper, who do you think are the exact types of people, businesses and grantors with the strongest reason to fund your mission?\nJust share what comes to mind.",
    "a1_deeper": "Beautiful.\nNow let's go a little deeper.\nWhen I say identify your ideal funders, I don't mean simply saying parents, businesses or foundations.\nWe need to get specific enough that your organization can actually go out and find these people.\nFor individuals, think about who has experienced the problem you're solving.\nWho knows somebody affected by it?\nWho strongly believes in the change you're trying to create?\nWho benefits when the people or community you serve become stronger?\nWho is already spending their time, attention or money around issues connected to your mission?\nThen look at businesses.\nWhich businesses serve the same people you serve?\nWho employs them?\nWho sells to them?\nWho benefits when this community becomes stronger?\nWhich companies already support issues connected to your work?\nThen look at grantors.\nWho already funds your issue?\nWho funds the population you serve?\nWho funds your geography, your type of program or the outcome you're trying to create?\nThe clearer you are about who you're looking for, the easier the rest of fundraising becomes.\nSo based on that, who specifically should your organization be looking for?",
    "approval_review": "Great.\nI've taken what you shared and cleaned it up so that your organization can actually use it.\nTake a look at both versions.\nIf the fine-tuned version captures what you mean, use it.\nIf you prefer exactly what you said, keep your original.",
    "a2_intro": "Now that we know who we're looking for, the next question is where do we find them?\nBecause knowing your ideal funders is only useful if your organization can consistently put those people in front of you.\nThis is where fundraising begins to become a system.\nYou shouldn't wake up every morning wondering, who are we going to contact today?\nYou should already know where your ideal funders congregate and have a process that consistently helps your organization find them.\nSo looking at the people, businesses and grantors you identified, where do you think you can consistently find them?",
    "a2_deeper": "Now think about it this way.\nIf you're looking for Christians, you go to the church.\nIf you're looking for Muslims, you go to the mosque.\nWhy?\nBecause that's where they congregate.\nThe same thing applies to fundraising.\nThe exact people you're trying to reach are already somewhere.\nThey belong to communities.\nThey work somewhere.\nThey attend events.\nThey join professional associations.\nThey use particular platforms.\nThey belong to churches, clubs, alumni networks, Facebook groups, LinkedIn communities and professional networks.\nBusinesses also congregate.\nYou find them through chambers, trade associations, business networks, industry events, directories and LinkedIn.\nAnd grantors congregate too.\nYou find them through funding databases, community foundations, funder networks, existing grantee lists, conferences, information sessions and the people responsible for their funding programs.\nYou don't need to search everywhere.\nYou need to know where your people already are.\nBased on that, where should your organization consistently go to find the funders you identified?",
    "a3_intro": "Now let's talk about attraction.\nBecause knowing where your funders are does not mean you need to spend every day chasing them.\nThere are things your organization can put out there that will attract the right people to you.\nThere are things you can put out there that will attract the right businesses.\nAnd there are ways of positioning your organization that can attract the attention of grantors as well.\nSo before we talk about what those things could be, what do you think your organization can offer that would make your ideal funders want to stop, pay attention and connect with you?",
    "a3_deeper": "Now let's go a little deeper.\nThink about your ideal funders.\nWhat can you put in front of them that is already valuable to them?\nIt could be a guide.\nIt could be a report.\nA checklist.\nA survey.\nAn assessment.\nA webinar.\nAn event.\nA community activity.\nA campaign.\nA useful resource.\nAn opportunity to share their opinion.\nOr simply an invitation to become part of something they already care about.\nFor businesses, sometimes the attraction is simply starting the right conversation around something both your organization and that business care about.\nFor grantors, attraction can mean understanding their priorities, attending their information sessions, asking good questions and making sure they know your organization before an application ever lands in front of them.\nThis is very important.\nYour first interaction does not always have to be an ask.\nThe first goal is to get the right people to notice you, connect with you and give you the opportunity to build a relationship.\nBased on that, what can your organization offer your potential funders that gives them a reason to move toward you before you ask them for money?",
    "a4_intro": "Now we get to the actual fundraising process.\nFinding somebody is not fundraising.\nAttracting them is not fundraising either.\nYou still need a process that moves that relationship towards financial support.\nAnd this is where many organizations get it wrong.\nThey meet somebody for the first time and immediately ask them for money.\nThat's not a fundraising system.\nThere needs to be a clear process.\nSo before I walk you through ours, once potential funders connect with your organization, how do you think you should raise money from them?",
    "a4_deeper": "Now, this is one of the most important things I want you to learn from this game.\nYou don't raise money by meeting somebody for the first time and immediately asking them for money.\nPeople usually need to know you, like you and trust you before they are ready to give.\nSo the fundraising process we use is very simple.\nKnow.\nLike.\nTrust.\nAsk.\nFollow up.\nSteward.\nFirst is KNOW.\nThe potential funder needs to discover your organization and understand what you do.\nThen LIKE.\nGive them reasons to stay connected, pay attention and become interested in the work.\nThen TRUST.\nThey need to see your impact.\nThey need to trust your leadership.\nThey need to believe your organization can actually create the change you say you're creating.\nThen you ASK.\nAt that point, you clearly invite them to financially support the mission.\nAnd if they don't give immediately, you FOLLOW UP.\nDon't just disappear.\nContinue the relationship.\nFind out what they need.\nGive them more information.\nContinue the conversation.\nThen when they give, you STEWARD them.\nThank them.\nShow them what their support made possible.\nKeep them connected.\nBecause the goal is not simply to get one donation.\nThe goal is to build a relationship that can continue to support the mission.\nAnd remember, this process may look different for individuals, businesses and grantors.\nA grantor may need to know your organization long before your application comes in.\nA business may need several conversations before a partnership or sponsorship opportunity makes sense.\nAn individual may need to experience your stories, events, content and impact before making that first gift.\nBut the principle remains the same.\nKnow.\nLike.\nTrust.\nAsk.\nFollow up.\nSteward.\nNow based on this process, how should your organization move your potential funders from first connection to financial support and an ongoing relationship?",
    "lead_free_complete": "Beautiful.\nYou have now built the foundation of your organization's fundraising strategy.\nYou identified who should fund your mission.\nWhere to find them.\nHow to attract them.\nAnd the process your organization can use to raise money from them.\nBut remember something.\nIf you build this alone, you're most likely going to manage it alone.\nThe real power comes when your board builds it with you.\nBecause the more people who play the game, the more ideas you have on how to raise money and the more support you have when raising money and building the system that drives the strategy.\nSo now, let's bring your board into the game.",
    "reality_intro": "Great.\nNow we know your fundraising direction.\nBut we're not going to build a strategy that ignores where your organization is today.\nBefore we build the complete working strategy, we need to understand what you already have.\nDo you already have individual donors?\nDo you have businesses supporting you?\nDo you already have grantors?\nWho is presently helping with fundraising?\nWhat technology, materials, resources and tools are already available?\nBecause once we know what you already have, we can identify what needs to be strengthened, what needs to be added and what system needs to be built around the strategy you just created.\nSo let's look at your present reality.",
    "reality_donors": "Let's start with individuals.\nTell us what already exists.\nYou don't need a perfect donor database.\nWe simply need to understand the individual donor relationships your organization already has so we can build from there.",
    "reality_businesses": "Now businesses.\nThink beyond businesses that have already given you money.\nInclude businesses that sponsor you, provide resources, employ people connected to your mission or already have a relationship with the organization.\nWe want to know what we can build from.",
    "reality_grantors": "Now let's look at grantors.\nTell us who already funds you or has funded you before.\nExisting funding relationships are valuable because your future grant strategy should not ignore the relationships you have already built.",
    "reality_team": "Now let's look at people.\nEvery fundraising strategy needs people to execute it.\nThat could be you.\nStaff.\nVolunteers.\nBoard members.\nA contractor.\nOr people who help occasionally.\nTell us who is already involved so we know the capacity you currently have.",
    "reality_resources": "Finally, tell us what your organization already has to work with.\nDo you have a CRM?\nAn email marketing tool?\nA donation platform?\nScheduling tools?\nFundraising messages?\nA case for support?\nProposals?\nImpact stories?\nReports?\nAnything your fundraising system can already use.\nWe don't want to recommend things you already have.\nWe want to understand what exists and identify what's missing.",
    "part_intro": "Now let's talk about you.\nA fundraising strategy cannot execute itself.\nSomebody has to help build the system.\nSomebody has to help run it.\nSomebody has to open doors.\nSomebody has to build relationships.\nSomebody has to ask.\nSomebody has to follow up.\nBut everybody does not have to do the same thing.\nAnd this is where organizations sometimes make a mistake.\nThey give every board member the same fundraising expectation, even when everybody has different strengths, relationships and experiences.\nWe don't want to do that.\nWe want you to tell us where you genuinely believe you can help.",
    "part_build": "First, let's look behind the scenes.\nRaising money is only one part of fundraising.\nSomebody also has to help build and manage the system that makes fundraising possible.\nThat could mean researching potential funders, recruiting people to help, maintaining the CRM, helping with technology, creating materials, organizing events, tracking follow-up or reviewing what's working and improving the strategy.\nLook through the options and choose the areas where you genuinely feel you can help.",
    "part_raise": "Now let's talk about actually raising money.\nAgain, everybody does not have to make the ask.\nMaybe your strength is making introductions.\nMaybe you can identify businesses.\nMaybe you know grantmakers.\nMaybe you're comfortable attending meetings.\nMaybe you're good at building relationships.\nMaybe you can make the ask.\nMaybe you would rather help follow up or steward people after they give.\nChoose the areas where you are genuinely willing to participate.",
    "part_time": "Now be realistic with this one.\nWe don't want to build a strategy around time you don't actually have.\nTell us what you can genuinely commit each month.\nIt is better to commit three hours and actually give three hours than commit ten and never have the time to do it.",
    "part_anything": "Before we finish, I want to give you one more opportunity to share anything we haven't asked.\nMaybe there's a person you know.\nA business.\nA grantor.\nAn opportunity.\nA fundraising idea.\nA concern.\nOr another way you believe you can help.\nIf something came to mind while playing the game, put it here.\nIf not, you can simply continue.",
    "board_complete": "Beautiful.\nYou're done.\nBut I want you to think about what you just did.\nYou didn't simply answer some fundraising questions.\nYou identified who should fund your organization's mission.\nYou thought about where those funders can consistently be found.\nYou identified ways to attract their attention.\nYou learned the process of moving potential funders through know, like, trust, ask, follow up and steward.\nAnd you told your organization exactly how you want to participate.\nYour ideas will now become part of the fundraising strategy your board builds together.\nAnd this is important.\nPeople who plan together execute together.\nBecause you were part of building the strategy, you now understand the thinking behind it and where you can help.\nWhen your board comes together, everyone's ideas will be brought into the same room so you can decide what the organization should prioritize.\nThank you for playing.\nYou're ready for Game Day or Game Night.",
    "lead_setup_complete": "Great.\nWe now have your fundraising direction, we understand what your organization already has and we know how you want to participate.\nThat's enough for us to begin building your working fundraising strategy.\nRemember, the strategy is the roadmap.\nIt tells you who you're raising money from, where to find them, how to attract them, the process to raise money from them and what your organization needs to execute.\nThe next step is bringing more people into the process.\nBecause the stronger the collective thinking behind the strategy, the stronger the strategy becomes.\nWe're taking you to your dashboard now.\nYour working strategy will be there as soon as it's ready, and you can continue preparing your board while we build it.",
}

